  {
   "n": 23,
   "type": "MCQ",
   "section": "Technical",
   "tags": "Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33",
   "html": "<p>Which of the following options are correct with respect to the following statement.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : A depth-first search of a directed graph always produces the same number of tree edges (i.e. independent of the order in which the vertices are provided and independent of the order of the adjacency lists).<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : If a topological sort exists for the vertices in a directed graph, then a DFS on the graph will produce no back edges.</p>",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "html": "<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is true only"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "html": "<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is true only"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "html": "Both are true"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "html": "None of the above"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "B"
   ],
   "natRange": null,
   "answerText": "B",
   "marks": {
    "pos": 1,
    "neg": 0.33
   },
   "solution": "<p>(b)<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is False. The DFS forest may contain different numbers of trees (and tree edges) depending on the starting vertex and upon the order in which vertices are searched.<br />\nConsider the example below. If the DFS starts at a, then it will visit <em>b</em> next, and (<em>a</em>, <em>b</em>) will become a tree edge. But if the DFS visits <em>b</em> first, then a and b become separate trees in the DFS forest, and (<em>a</em>, <em>b</em>) becomes a cross edge.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is True. Both parts of the statement hold if and only if the graph is acyclic.</p>"
  },
